.Pp
This machine was installed when the machine owner key was a key of its
own, not the authority's root: the ZFS module is signed by that key,
which is enrolled, and the authority is not.
See
.Sx CAVEATS .
.Ss Turning it on
Not run for this page:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-secure-boot enable
# then reboot, choose "Enroll MOK" in MokManager, give the password,
# reboot, and turn Secure Boot on in the firmware
.Ed
.Ss Mistakes
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-secure-boot enable
[kldload-secure-boot] error: must run as root
# (exited 1)
$ kldload-secure-boot frob
kldload-secure-boot: unknown command: frob
\&...
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mokutil 1 ,
.Xr sbsign 1 ,
.Xr dkms 8 ,
.Xr kldload-ca 8 ,
.Xr kldload-tpm-seal 8
.Sh HISTORY
It replaced a comment in 1.0.5 that pointed at a tool which did not exist.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page:
.Cm status
died half way on every run;
.Cm enable
stripped the Microsoft signature from shim on the EFI fallback path and
signed it with the kldload key, which the firmware does not trust, so that
path would no longer boot with Secure Boot on \(em while ZFSBootMenu, where
the installer puts it, was never re-signed; it signed a compressed ZFS
module in place, which leaves it corrupt, with success reported; and a key
already queued was not recognised, so it was queued again.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
On a machine installed while the machine owner key was separate from the
authority, like the one above,
.Cm enable
replaces the DKMS key with the authority's and signs the ZFS module with
it at once, although the firmware does not trust that key until the
enrolment is confirmed at the next boot.
Skip MokManager then, and modules signed from here on will not load with
Secure Boot on.
.Pp
One key signs both the machine's TLS certificates and its kernel modules;
see
.Xr kldload-ca 8 .
.Pp
The initramfs keeps the ZFS module it was built with;
.Cm enable
does not rebuild it.
On a machine whose modules are compressed, the ZFS module is signed with
the new key only when DKMS next builds it:
.Ql dkms autoinstall -k Ns Ar kernel ,
or the next kernel.
